Design details:

Types:
```ts
export interface LineageEdge {
  readonly child: IdentityVersionID;
  readonly parent: IdentityVersionID;
  readonly relation: LineageRelationType;
}

export interface LineageGraph {
  readonly nodes: readonly IdentityVersionID[];
  readonly edges: readonly DeepReadonly<LineageEdge>[];
  readonly unresolvedParents: readonly (readonly [IdentityVersionID, readonly IdentityVersionID[]])[];
  readonly cycles: readonly (readonly IdentityVersionID[])[];
}
```

build:
```ts
export function buildLineageGraph(registry: Registry): LineageGraph {
  const versions = registry.versions();
  const nodes = versions.map(v => v.id).sort(compareId); // dedupe? registry assumed consistent; but "total for inconsistent input" - duplicate ids? Use unique sorted set.
  const nodeSet = new Set(nodes);
  const edges: LineageEdge[] = [];
  for (const version of versions) {
    for (const link of version.lineage) {
      edges.push({ child: version.id, parent: link.parent_version_id, relation: link.relation });
    }
  }
  edges.sort(compareEdge);
  // unresolved
  const missingByChild = new Map<IdentityVersionID, Set<IdentityVersionID>>();
  for (const edge of edges) {
    if (!nodeSet.has(edge.parent)) {
      const set = missingByChild.get(edge.child) ?? new Set();
      set.add(edge.parent);
      missingByChild.set(edge.child, set);
    }
  }
  const unresolvedParents = [...missingByChild.entries()]
    .sort(([a],[b]) => compareId(a,b))
    .map(([child, parents]) => [child, [...parents].sort(compareId)] as const);
  const cycles = findCyclicComponents(nodes, edges);
  return deepFreeze({ nodes, edges, unresolvedParents, cycles });
}
```
Nodes dedupe: use sorted unique. If duplicate registry ids, edges from both descriptors; node set dedupes; "total for inconsistent input". Fine.

Sorting comparator: strings are branded; compare via `<`/`>`.
```ts
function compareIds(a: IdentityVersionID, b: IdentityVersionID): number {
  return a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0;
}
```
Edge sort: child, then parent, then relation.

SCC detection:
- Adjacency: Map<IdentityVersionID, IdentityVersionID[]>, dedupe parents per child (internal only).
  Include self-edges. For SCC, self-loop detection separately.
- Tarjan iterative or recursive. Graph size could be large; recursion risk. Implement iterative Tarjan? More complex. Alternative Kosaraju iterative:
  1. Build adjacency deduped, and reverse adjacency.
  2. Iterative DFS to compute finish order over sorted nodes and sorted neighbors.
  3. Process nodes in reverse finish order, iterative DFS on reverse graph to collect SCCs.
  Deterministic.
- Then for each SCC:
  - size > 1 → cyclic.
  - size === 1 → cyclic if self-edge (parent === child).
- Sort nodes within component, sort components by first node lexicographic (or joined key).

Implementation with iterative DFS:

```ts
function sortedAdjacency(nodes, edges): Map<IdentityVersionID, IdentityVersionID[]> {
  const adj = new Map<IdentityVersionID, Set<IdentityVersionID>>();
  for (const id of nodes) adj.set(id, new Set());
  for (const edge of edges) {
    // only edges from known child? All children are registry descriptors, so nodes contains child. But be safe: if (!adj.has(edge.child)) adj.set(edge.child, new Set());
    adj.get(edge.child)?.add(edge.parent);
    // unresolved parent may not be a node; don't add to adjacency keys? For reverse graph, it's not a node; cycles can't include missing parent. Skip unknown parents.
  }
  const out = new Map<...>();
  for (const [id, set] of adj) out.set(id, [...set].sort(compareIds));
  return out;
}
```
If child always in nodes (from descriptor), fine. Parent may be missing; adjacency includes edge to missing parent; during DFS, if neighbor not in adjacency (missing), skip. For SCC, only nodes are registry versions.

Kosaraju:
```ts
function findCyclicComponents(nodes, edges): readonly (readonly IdentityVersionID[])[] {
  const adjacency = buildAdjacency(nodes, edges);
  const order: IdentityVersionID[] = [];
  const visited = new Set<IdentityVersionID>();
  for (const start of nodes) { // nodes sorted
    if (visited.has(start)) continue;
    // iterative DFS with stack of [node, index]
    const stack: Array<{node: IdentityVersionID; next: number}> = [{node:start, next:0}];
    visited.add(start);
    while (stack.length > 0) {
      const frame = stack[stack.length-1];
      const neighbors = adjacency.get(frame.node) ?? [];
      if (frame.next < neighbors.length) {
        const neighbor = neighbors[frame.next];
        frame.next += 1;
        if (!visited.has(neighbor) && adjacency.has(neighbor)) {
          visited.add(neighbor);
          stack.push({node: neighbor, next: 0});
        }
      } else {
        order.push(frame.node);
        stack.pop();
      }
    }
  }
  // reverse graph
  const reverse = new Map<IdentityVersionID, IdentityVersionID[]>();
  for (const id of nodes) reverse.set(id, []);
  for (const [child, parents] of adjacency) {
    for (const parent of parents) {
      if (reverse.has(parent)) reverse.get(parent)!.push(child);
    }
  }
  for (const list of reverse.values()) list.sort(compareIds);

  const components: IdentityVersionID[][] = [];
  const assigned = new Set<IdentityVersionID>();
  for (let i = order.length - 1; i >= 0; i -= 1) {
    const start = order[i];
    if (assigned.has(start)) continue;
    const component: IdentityVersionID[] = [];
    const stack = [start];
    assigned.add(start);
    while (stack.length > 0) {
      const node = stack.pop()!;
      component.push(node);
      for (const child of reverse.get(node) ?? []) {
        if (!assigned.has(child)) { assigned.add(child); stack.push(child); }
      }
    }
    component.sort(compareIds);
    if (isCyclicComponent(component, adjacency)) components.push(component);
  }
  components.sort((a,b) => compareIds(a[0], b[0])); // each nonempty
  return components;
}

function isCyclicComponent(component, adjacency): boolean {
  if (component.length > 1) return true;
  const only = component[0];
  return (adjacency.get(only) ?? []).includes(only);
}
```
Self-loop: component size 1, adjacency includes itself → cyclic.

Note: adjacency keys include nodes; for missing parent, `adjacency.has(parent)` false; but reverse built only for nodes. In first DFS, neighbor missing → skip (`adjacency.has(neighbor)` check). Good.

Duplicate edges: adjacency dedupes internally; exposed edges keep duplicates.

DeepFreeze graph: `deepFreeze({ nodes, edges, unresolvedParents, cycles })`. Type: arrays of branded ids (primitives preserved by DeepReadonly fix), edges objects mapped readonly, tuples mapped. Return type LineageGraph; deepFreeze returns DeepReadonly<...> which should be assignable? `LineageGraph` fields are already readonly; tuples `readonly [id, readonly id[]]`. `DeepReadonly` on tuple: T extends array → readonly DeepReadonly<A>[] — tuple becomes array, losing tuple type! Hmm: `DeepReadonly<readonly [A, B]>` where tuple extends `(infer A)[]` → `readonly DeepReadonly<A>[]` generic array, so `unresolvedParents` type would become `readonly (readonly (IdentityVersionID | readonly IdentityVersionID[])[])[]`? Actually conditional on union element type yields union array. Not assignable to tuple type.

To avoid, construct the graph with tuple type and deepFreeze with cast:
```ts
return deepFreeze(graph) as LineageGraph;
```
Or freeze manually with Object.freeze per level. Simpler: build typed `graph: LineageGraph`, then `return deepFreeze(graph) as LineageGraph;` Since deepFreeze returns DeepReadonly<T>; cast after freeze. This is internal and safe.

But DeepReadonly<T> for tuple arrays might be used in types elsewhere? Fine.

Alternatively type cycles as `readonly (readonly IdentityVersionID[])[]` etc. and unresolved as `ReadonlyArray<readonly [IdentityVersionID, readonly IdentityVersionID[]]>`; deepFreeze maps tuple to array; cast.

Implementation note: deepFreeze on branded strings leaves them.

