One failure: existing assessments are not frozen (test passes plain fixture). `registerAssessment` returns `[...existing, registered]`; existing items are caller objects (not frozen). The requirement says returned array and all existing assessments? "existing records remain unchanged" not necessarily frozen. Test asserts every item frozen — too strong unless we deep-copy existing too. Should the returned registry freeze existing items? If we copy existing, we'd break reference equality with caller (not required). For ownership, returned list should be frozen and elements should be owned. We can deep-copy existing as well, then freeze. But then caller's existing records won't be the same references; that's okay (copy-on-write). However, predecessors must be preserved byte-for-byte; copying preserves. Let's do:

```ts
const copiedExisting = deepCopy(existing);
if (!copiedExisting.ok) return copiedExisting;
const frozenExisting = deepFreeze(copiedExisting.value);
const registered = deepFreeze(copied.value);
...
return { ok: true, value: Object.freeze([...frozenExisting, registered]) };
```
But cycle detection should use existing (same ids) — can use frozenExisting. Deep-copying existing on each registration is fine for reference implementation.

Then all items frozen. Predecessor deep-equal. Test passes. Also no mutation.

Do it. Also `snapshot(existing)` unchanged.

Apply.

